
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I present the design — it's about how those [3mother[0m forms actually reach the identifier.

  Right now [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is loaded with a plain [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m. There are no modules, no bundler, and no dependencies.
  So "other forms need it too" requires picking how code gets shared between files, and that choice is structural.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the shared identity code is a real module or a global.

  - [1mES modules[0m ([38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, a new [38;5;153msrc/session.js[39m with [38;5;153mimport[39m/[38;5;153mexport[39m) — proper encapsulation, explicit
    dependencies, no build step, and each future form just imports what it needs. One real cost: [38;5;153mtype="module"[39m is
    fetched under CORS rules, so [38;5;153mindex.html[39m stops working when opened directly from disk as a [38;5;153mfile://[39m URL — you'd need
    to serve it ([38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m or similar). If double-clicking the HTML file is part of how you work, this
    changes that.
  - [1mGlobal namespace object[0m (a second [38;5;153m<script src="session.js">[39m defining [38;5;153mwindow.Session[39m) — zero setup, keeps [38;5;153mfile://[39m
    working, every later script sees it automatically. Cost: no encapsulation, load order matters, and anything on the
    page can overwrite it. It's the pattern this file is already in.
  - [1mAdd a bundler[0m — buys nothing here that ES modules don't, and adds a toolchain to a repo with zero dependencies.
    Listing it to reject it.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m ES modules. You're building a layer that multiple future consumers depend on, and that's exactly
  the case where implicit global ordering turns into bugs. The [38;5;153mfile://[39m tradeoff is the only thing I'd want you to
  confirm, since it's a change to how you open the page.
